                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "stile",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6b2be117-8f25-3041-9693-7e5a22e98243",
                                        "entry_id": "a4253638-94f0-3353-a874-c4b3b1506509",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A set of one or more steps surmounting a fence or wall, or a narrow gate or contrived passage through a fence or wall, which in either case allows people but not livestock to pass."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0f0eeee9-8c6f-4eef-9b20-38be2cd95147",
                                                "sense_id": "6b2be117-8f25-3041-9693-7e5a22e98243",
                                                "text": "'Twas very true what Greening said; for of a summer evening I would take the path that led up Weatherbeech Hill, behind the Manor; both because 'twas a walk that had a good prospect in itself, and also a sweet charm for me, namely, the hope of seeing Grace Maskew. And there I often sat upon the stile that ends the path and opens on the down, and watched the old half-ruined house below; and sometimes saw white-frocked Gracie walking on the terrace in the evening sun, and sometimes in returning passed her window near enough to wave a greeting."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8b78c9dc-1c88-3730-a292-5b90b6f57d4b",
                                        "entry_id": "a4253638-94f0-3353-a874-c4b3b1506509",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A vertical component of a frame or panel, such as that of a door, window, or ladder."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "stile",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b3ee3800-b3e8-3ecb-9d78-13097a65f30a",
                                        "entry_id": "e566cb14-229a-3d47-83ee-eaddbabf7b9c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Obsolete form of style."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c7c2b57b-93ac-47c3-9aa9-25da6456d04f",
                                                "sense_id": "b3ee3800-b3e8-3ecb-9d78-13097a65f30a",
                                                "text": "May I not write in such a ſtile as this? / In ſuch a method too, and yet not miſs / Mine end, thy good? why may it not be done?"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7f554f5c-d616-490a-96a4-4d289bd702b5",
                                                "sense_id": "b3ee3800-b3e8-3ecb-9d78-13097a65f30a",
                                                "text": "Every Printing-houſe is by the Cuſtom of Time out of mind, called a Chappel; and all the Workmen that belong to it are Members of the Chappel: and the Oldeſt Freeman is the Father of the Chappel. I ſuppoſe the ſtile was originally conferred upon it by the courteſie of ſome great Churchman, or men, (doubtleſs when Chappels were in more veneration than of late years they have been here in England) who for the Books of Divinity that proceeded from a Printing-houſe, gave it the Reverend Title of Chappel."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cac601de-6635-4496-94ff-2d7de05e5e2a",
                                                "sense_id": "b3ee3800-b3e8-3ecb-9d78-13097a65f30a",
                                                "text": "Laſt of all fit a Triangular Iron, whoſe angular point being laid to the Center of the Dyal Plane, one ſide muſt agree with the Subſtilar Line, and its other ſide with the Stilar Line; ſo is the Stile made. And this Stile you muſt erect perpendicularly over the Subſtilar Line on the Dyal Plane, and there fix it. Then is your Dyal finiſhed."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "stile",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9ba3ca4d-5ad5-3734-b77b-690d2bbbbcfd",
                                        "entry_id": "6e484544-73a4-3f0e-8204-89941b9e1d04",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Obsolete form of style."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "094862da-f6df-42bc-bd2b-1f6a018754fe",
                                                "sense_id": "9ba3ca4d-5ad5-3734-b77b-690d2bbbbcfd",
                                                "text": "They are graciously and lovingly received, the Mayor hath thanks for his late bloody Act, which was stiled and excellent piece of Justice"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4f7997b2-271d-4d4d-a665-98cfbfd24a82",
                                                "sense_id": "9ba3ca4d-5ad5-3734-b77b-690d2bbbbcfd",
                                                "text": "This Fellow, I ſay, ſtiled himſelf a Lawyer, but was indeed a moſt vile Petty-fogger, without Senſe or Knowledge of any Kind; one of thoſe who may be termed Train-bearers to the Law; [...]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bce03d50-ed73-49bc-9091-4b35047d735a",
                                                "sense_id": "9ba3ca4d-5ad5-3734-b77b-690d2bbbbcfd",
                                                "text": "Marianne's preserver, as Margaret, with more elegance than precision, stiled Willoughby, called at the cottage early the next morning to make his personal inquiries."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
